    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "uk29tql",
      "content": "<strong>UNIT 1 — QUANTITATIVE APTITUDE</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jk7jad8",
      "content": "Quantitative Aptitude tests numerical ability and mathematical reasoning. PSU CBT exams typically have 25–40 questions from this section. The key topics are: Number System, Percentage, Ratio &amp; Proportion, Average, Profit &amp; Loss, Simple &amp; Compound Interest, Time–Work, Time–Speed–Distance, Algebra, Geometry, Data Interpretation, and Mensuration."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "wepwrng",
      "content": "<strong>1.1  Number System</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "atrzrwo",
      "content": "A number system defines how numbers are classified. The most important classifications for PSU exams are:"
    },
    {
      "id": "4ez64fq",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Natural Numbers: 1, 2, 3, 4 … (positive integers excluding zero)",
        "Whole Numbers: 0, 1, 2, 3 … (natural numbers including zero)",
        "Integers: … –3, –2, –1, 0, 1, 2, 3 … (positive and negative whole numbers)",
        "Rational Numbers: Numbers expressible as p/q where q ≠ 0. E.g. 3/4, 0.5, –2",
        "Irrational Numbers: Non-terminating, non-repeating decimals. E.g. √2, π",
        "Prime Numbers: Divisible only by 1 and itself. E.g. 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13 …",
        "Composite Numbers: Having more than two factors. E.g. 4, 6, 8, 9 …"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "q6kp65t",
      "content": "<strong>Divisibility Rules</strong>"
    },
    {
      "id": "idgi3qi",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Divisor</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Rule</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Example</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>2</p>",
            "<p>Last digit is 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8</p>",
            "<p>128 → last digit 8 ✓</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>3</p>",
            "<p>Sum of digits divisible by 3</p>",
            "<p>123 → 1+2+3=6 ✓</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>4</p>",
            "<p>Last two digits divisible by 4</p>",
            "<p>316 → 16÷4=4 ✓</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>5</p>",
            "<p>Last digit is 0 or 5</p>",
            "<p>235 → last digit 5 ✓</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>6</p>",
            "<p>Divisible by both 2 and 3</p>",
            "<p>312 → even &amp; 3+1+2=6 ✓</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>7</p>",
            "<p>Double last digit, subtract from rest; result divisible by 7</p>",
            "<p>203 → 20–6=14 ✓</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>8</p>",
            "<p>Last three digits divisible by 8</p>",
            "<p>1,024 → 024÷8=3 ✓</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>9</p>",
            "<p>Sum of digits divisible by 9</p>",
            "<p>729 → 7+2+9=18 ✓</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>11</p>",
            "<p>Alternate digit sum difference divisible by 11</p>",
            "<p>121 → 1–2+1=0 ✓</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "fflxezb",
      "content": "<strong>LCM and HCF</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "moox8rk",
      "content": "HCF (Highest Common Factor): The greatest number that divides two or more numbers exactly. Also called GCD. LCM (Lowest Common Multiple): The smallest number divisible by two or more given numbers."
    },
    {
      "id": "0u22ajb",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Key Formula: </strong>HCF × LCM = Product of the two numbers</p><p><strong>Example: </strong>HCF(12,18) = 6 | LCM(12,18) = 36 | Check: 6 × 36 = 216 = 12 × 18 ✓</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "vdfy8hd",
      "content": "<strong>1.2  Percentage</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vdoxwpl",
      "content": "Percentage means \"per hundred\". It is used to compare quantities and express fractions in a standard form. PSU exams frequently combine percentage with profit/loss and data interpretation."
    },
    {
      "id": "377xu7h",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Expression</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>x% of y</p>",
            "<p>(x/100) × y</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>a is what % of b</p>",
            "<p>(a/b) × 100</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>% increase</p>",
            "<p>[(New – Old)/Old] × 100</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>% decrease</p>",
            "<p>[(Old – New)/Old] × 100</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Successive % change (a% then b%)</p>",
            "<p>a + b + (ab/100)</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "kf1mazy",
      "content": "<strong>Solved Examples</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "q887upv",
      "content": "<strong>Example 1: </strong>A salary of Rs. 25,000 is increased by 20%. Find the new salary."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rh0b1ks",
      "content": "<strong>Solution: </strong>Increase = 20% of 25,000 = 5,000. New salary = 25,000 + 5,000 = Rs. 30,000"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xt3xe23",
      "content": "<strong>Example 2: </strong>A price is first increased by 10% and then decreased by 10%. Find net % change."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1j7ojvm",
      "content": "<strong>Solution: </strong>Net % change = 10 + (–10) + (10 × –10)/100 = –1%. Net decrease of 1%."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "bxklurb",
      "content": "<strong>1.3  Ratio &amp; Proportion</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4un3kq7",
      "content": "A ratio compares two quantities of the same type. A proportion states that two ratios are equal."
    },
    {
      "id": "8g4wtmw",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "If a:b = c:d, then ad = bc (cross multiplication rule)",
        "If a:b:c = 2:3:5, and total is T, then a = 2T/10, b = 3T/10, c = 5T/10",
        "Compounded ratio of a:b and c:d = ac:bd",
        "Duplicate ratio of a:b = a²:b² | Sub-duplicate = √a:√b"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u8v1ktl",
      "content": "<strong>Example: </strong>Divide Rs. 4,500 in the ratio 2:3:4."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hvcrxbt",
      "content": "<strong>Solution: </strong>Total parts = 9. Shares = (2/9)×4500=1000, (3/9)×4500=1500, (4/9)×4500=2000"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "9y8xuje",
      "content": "<strong>1.4  Average</strong>"
    },
    {
      "id": "hjqv3fl",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Average = Sum of all observations / Number of observations</strong></p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5fjuv5y",
      "content": "If the average of n numbers is A and a new number x is added, new average = (nA + x)/(n+1). If the average of n numbers is A and one number x is removed, new average = (nA – x)/(n–1)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7um7703",
      "content": "<strong>Weighted Average: </strong>= (w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + … + wₙxₙ) / (w₁ + w₂ + … + wₙ)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "4u47cnd",
      "content": "<strong>1.5  Profit &amp; Loss</strong>"
    },
    {
      "id": "tand5xx",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Term</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Profit</p>",
            "<p>Selling Price – Cost Price (when SP &gt; CP)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Loss</p>",
            "<p>Cost Price – Selling Price (when CP &gt; SP)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Profit %</p>",
            "<p>(Profit / CP) × 100</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Loss %</p>",
            "<p>(Loss / CP) × 100</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>SP (when profit%)</p>",
            "<p>CP × (100 + Profit%) / 100</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>SP (when loss%)</p>",
            "<p>CP × (100 – Loss%) / 100</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Marked Price &amp; Discount</p>",
            "<p>Discount = MP – SP | Discount% = (Discount/MP) × 100</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Successive Discounts d1 and d2</p>",
            "<p>Net SP = MP × (1–d1/100) × (1–d2/100)</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "0pyqjoj",
      "content": "<strong>1.6  Simple &amp; Compound Interest</strong>"
    },
    {
      "id": "o46gxw4",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Variables</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Simple Interest (SI)</p>",
            "<p>SI = (P × R × T) / 100</p>",
            "<p>P=Principal, R=Rate%, T=Time(yrs)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Amount (SI)</p>",
            "<p>A = P + SI</p>",
            "<p>A = P(1 + RT/100)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Compound Interest (CI)</p>",
            "<p>A = P(1 + R/100)ⁿ</p>",
            "<p>n = number of years</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>CI (half-yearly)</p>",
            "<p>A = P(1 + R/200)²ⁿ</p>",
            "<p>R halved, period doubled</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>CI (quarterly)</p>",
            "<p>A = P(1 + R/400)⁴ⁿ</p>",
            "<p>R quartered, period ×4</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Difference (CI–SI) for 2 years</p>",
            "<p>P(R/100)²</p>",
            "<p>Useful shortcut</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "qd3m561",
      "content": "<strong>1.7  Time, Work &amp; Pipes</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ftv6gqi",
      "content": "If A can do a work in x days, A's one day work = 1/x. If A and B together can do work in T days, then 1/T = 1/x + 1/y."
    },
    {
      "id": "waxsjbl",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Work done = Efficiency × Time",
        "If A is twice as efficient as B and they work together to finish in T days: T = 2x/3 where x = days A takes alone",
        "Pipe filling: If pipe A fills in x hrs and pipe B empties in y hrs, net rate = 1/x – 1/y"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "845r6yd",
      "content": "<strong>Example: </strong>A can do a work in 12 days, B in 18 days. How long will they take together?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "j1c0n5p",
      "content": "<strong>Solution: </strong>Together: 1/12 + 1/18 = 3/36 + 2/36 = 5/36. Days = 36/5 = 7.2 days"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ocrdefl",
      "content": "<strong>1.8  Speed, Distance &amp; Time</strong>"
    },
    {
      "id": "ud8ktcv",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Distance = Speed × Time  |  Speed = Distance/Time  |  Time = Distance/Speed</strong></p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "jk8cs99",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Concept</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Average speed (equal distances)</p>",
            "<p>2s₁s₂/(s₁+s₂)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Relative speed – same direction</p>",
            "<p>|s₁ – s₂|</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Relative speed – opposite direction</p>",
            "<p>s₁ + s₂</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Train crossing a pole</p>",
            "<p>Time = Length of train / Speed</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Train crossing a platform</p>",
            "<p>Time = (Length of train + Length of platform) / Speed</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Boat downstream</p>",
            "<p>Speed = u + v (u=boat speed, v=stream speed)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Boat upstream</p>",
            "<p>Speed = u – v</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Speed in still water</p>",
            "<p>(Downstream + Upstream) / 2</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Stream speed</p>",
            "<p>(Downstream – Upstream) / 2</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "peityrt",
      "content": "<strong>1.9  Data Interpretation</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "q4kpkjk",
      "content": "Data Interpretation (DI) is one of the highest-weightage topics in PSU exams. Questions are based on Bar Charts, Line Graphs, Pie Charts, and Tables. Key approach: Read the question carefully, identify the relevant data, apply the correct formula, and avoid calculation errors."
    },
    {
      "id": "i0e8366",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>DI Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Key Skill Required</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Bar Chart</p>",
            "<p>Compare heights, calculate % change between years</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Line Graph</p>",
            "<p>Identify trends, calculate rate of change</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Pie Chart</p>",
            "<p>Convert degrees/% to actual values using totals</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Table</p>",
            "<p>Multi-step calculations, finding ratios and averages</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Mixed Graph</p>",
            "<p>Combine two charts; cross-reference data</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "hr3rnj3",
      "content": "<strong>Sample DI Set</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "baslrc9",
      "content": "The following table shows the production (in thousand tonnes) of five PSUs over 5 years:"
    },
    {
      "id": "rqql2ga",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>PSU / Year</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>2019</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>2020</strong></p>",
